## 7. Static vs dynamic routing

### Static routing

Routes manually entered into a routing table by a network administrator.
Simple and predictable but does not adapt automatically if a link fails. Used
in small networks or for specific fixed paths.

```
Advantages:    predictable, no protocol overhead, secure
Disadvantages: does not self-heal, labour-intensive at scale
```

### Dynamic routing

Routes learned and shared automatically between routers using routing
protocols. The routing table builds and updates itself — if a link fails,
an alternative path is found automatically.

```
Advantages:    self-healing, scales to large networks
Disadvantages: protocol overhead, more complex to configure
```

### Common dynamic routing protocols

| Protocol | Full name                                  | Use case                              |
|----------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
| RIP      | Routing Information Protocol               | Small, simple networks                |
| OSPF     | Open Shortest Path First                   | Enterprise networks                   |
| EIGRP    | Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol | Cisco networks                        |
| BGP      | Border Gateway Protocol                    | Routing between networks on the public internet |

### BGP — Border Gateway Protocol

The dynamic routing protocol that holds the public internet together. Used by
ISPs (Internet Service Providers) and major networks to advertise their address
blocks to each other and determine paths across the global internet. Every
major network on the internet runs BGP.

