## 8. NAT and PAT

### NAT (Network Address Translation)

A process running on a router at the boundary between a private network and
the public internet. Translates private source IP addresses to the router's
public IP address as traffic leaves, and reverses the translation when
responses return — allowing many private devices to share one public IP address.

```
Private side                  Router (NAT)              Public internet
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
192.168.1.5  ──► packet ──►  src: 192.168.1.5       ──►  41.90.64.22
                              translated to 41.90.64.22
Web server   ◄── response ◄─  dst: 41.90.64.22       ◄──  41.90.64.22
                              translated to 192.168.1.5
```

The router maintains a **NAT translation table** to track which private device
each outgoing connection belongs to, so responses are routed back correctly.

### PAT (Port Address Translation)

The most common form of NAT, also called **NAT overload**. Extends NAT by
using unique source port numbers to track multiple simultaneous connections
from different private devices — all sharing one public IP address.

Each connection is identified by the combination of public IP address and port
number, allowing the router to route responses back to the correct private
device.

```
Private device        NAT translation          Public internet
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
192.168.1.5:51234  →  41.90.64.22:51234       (laptop browsing)
192.168.1.7:51235  →  41.90.64.22:51235       (phone browsing)
192.168.1.9:51236  →  41.90.64.22:51236       (TV streaming)
```

All three devices share one public IP — differentiated by port number.

### NAT vs PAT

| Feature             | NAT                          | PAT                              |
|---------------------|------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| Translation unit    | IP address only              | IP address + port number         |
| Devices supported   | One private per public IP    | Many private per public IP       |
| Common use          | Static server mappings       | Home and office networks         |
| Also known as       | Basic NAT / static NAT       | NAT overload / dynamic NAT       |

