## 9. The full picture — data leaving your machine

When you type a URL into your browser and hit enter, here is what happens at
the network layer:

```
1. DNS resolves the hostname to a destination IP address (section 5)
2. Your machine checks: is the destination on my subnet?
   → No → forward to default gateway
3. ARP resolves the default gateway's MAC address
4. Frame is sent to the switch → switch forwards to the router port
5. Router strips the frame, reads the IP header
6. Router consults routing table → longest prefix match
7. NAT translates your private IP to the public IP
8. Packet leaves your network toward the destination
9. Each router along the path repeats steps 5 and 6
10. Destination server receives the packet, responds
11. Response travels back through the same routers
12. NAT translates the public IP back to your private IP
13. Your machine receives the response
```

